.TXT 4 11 310 0
Cg a62.000000,61.875000,563
{\rtf1\ansi \deff0
{\fonttbl
{\f0\fnil Arial;}
{\f1\fnil Symbol;}
}
{\plain {P}{rocessing radar signals was 
o}{ne of the earliest applications of DSP
 and it remains an important spur to DSP
 development. The early radar systems transmitted
 a pulse p(t) and then received the scaled
 and delayed pulse, }{\f1 s }{p(t-}{\f1 
t}{\fs16 \dn6 o}{), reflected by an isolated
 object. The measurement of the }{\b delay}{
 }{\f1 t}{\fs16 \dn6 o}{ gives the }{\b 
range}{ R}{\fs16 \dn6 o}{ by the relation
 }{\f1 t}{\fs16 \dn6 o}{ = 2R}{\fs16 \dn6 
o}{/c , where c is the velocity of light.
 The scale }{\f1 s}{ also depends on range,
 but less sensitively.}}
}
.TXT 16 0 311 0
Cg a61.750000,61.750000,146
{\rtf1\ansi \deff0
{\fonttbl
{\f0\fnil Arial;}
{\f1\fnil Symbol;}
}
{\plain {For a variety of reasons- such 
as efficiency of antennas and spectrum availability-
 the }{\b pulse}{ is a }{\b narrowband signal}{
 of the form}}
}
.EQN 6 6 312 0
{0:p}NAME({0:t}NAME):{0:f.m}NAME({0:t}NAME)*{0:cos}NAME({0:\n.c}NAME*{0:t}NAME+{0:\f}NAME({0:t}NAME))
.TXT 4 -6 313 0
Cg a59.000000,62.000000,206
{\rtf1\ansi \deff0
{\fonttbl
{\f0\fnil Arial;}
{\f1\fnil Symbol;}
}
{\plain {where }{\f1 n}{\fs16 \dn6 c}{ is
 the}{\b  radiation frequency}{, }{\b RF}{,
 and f}{\fs16 \dn6 m}{(t) and }{\f1 f}{(t)
 are, respectively, the}{\b  }{\b0 pulse}{\b 
 amplitude }{and}{\b  phase modulations}{.}}
}
.TXT 7 0 314 0
Cg a61.500000,61.875000,1037
{\rtf1\ansi \deff0
{\fonttbl
{\f0\fnil Arial;}
{\f1\fnil Symbol;}
}
{\plain {T}{o process the received radar
 signal, it is (i) translated in frequency
 by multiplying it by cos(}{\f1 n}{\fs16 
\dn6 c}{t) and a copy of it by sin(}{\f1 
n}{\fs16 \dn6 c}{t), the }{\b inphase}{ 
and }{\b quadrature carriers}{ generated
 by a}{\b  coherent}{ }{\b local oscillator
 (LO)}{\b0 ,}{\b0  and (ii) each resulting
 waveform is lowpass filtered to remove 
the double harmonic}{\b0 . The result is
 that the radar data is now the pair of 
waveforms (}{\f1 \b0 s}{\b0  f}{\fs16 \dn6 
\b0 m}{\b0 (t-}{\f1 \b0 t}{\fs16 \dn6 \b0 
o}{\b0 )cos(}{\f1 \b0 f}{\b0 (t-}{\f1 \b0 
t}{\fs16 \dn6 \b0 o}{\b0 )-}{\f1 \b0 q}{\b0 
) ,}{\f1 \b0 s}{\b0  f}{\fs16 \dn6 \b0 m}{\b0 
(t-}{\f1 \b0 t}{\fs16 \dn6 \b0 o}{\b0 )sin(}{\f1 
\b0 f}{\b0 (t-}{\f1 \b0 t}{\fs16 \dn6 \b0 
o}{\b0 )-}{\f1 \b0 q}{\b0 ) ) , where }{\f1 
\b0 q}{\b0 =}{\f1 \b0 n}{\fs16 \dn6 \b0 
c}{\f1 \b0 t}{\fs16 \dn6 \b0 o}{\b0  is 
a phase and }{\f1 \b0 s}{\b0  is an amplitude.
  Any such ordered pair of real functions
 can be taken as the real and imaginary 
parts of a }{\b complex representation }}
}
