{\rtf1\ansi \deff0
{\fonttbl
{\f0\fnil Arial;}
{\f1\fnil Symbol;}
}
{\plain {Our radar processing problem is
 twofold. First, we must decide if the signal
 is present in the data- this is the }{\b 
detection problem}{. If the signal is present
 then, secondly, we must estimate the delay-
 this is the}{\b  estimation problem}{.}}
}
.TXT 9 0 331 0
Cg a59.625000,62.000000,526
{\rtf1\ansi \deff0
{\fonttbl
{\f0\fnil Arial;}
{\f1\fnil Symbol;}
}
{\plain {What shall the radar processor 
be? There are several ways to arrive at 
the usual processor form but we choose an
 intuitive argument. First, let us address
 the detection problem, assuming for the
 moment that the parameters (b,}{\f1 t}{\fs16 
\dn6 o}{) are known and that }{\f1 t}{\fs16 
\dn6 o}{ = 0 and b=1. We know by now that
 a filter can partially eliminate the noise}{,
 so }{it is reasonable to take a linear 
filter }{with IR h(t)}{ }{a}{s the first
 stage of the radar processor}{, forming
 the partially processed data}}
}
.EQN 18 7 333 0
(-{0:\¥}NAME&{0:\¥}NAME`{0:z}NAME({0:s}NAME)*{0:h}NAME({0:t}NAME-{0:s}NAME)&{0:s}NAME)
