Cg a59.375000,61.000000,201
{\rtf1\ansi \deff0
{\fonttbl
{\f0\fnil Arial;}
{\f1\fnil Symbol;}
}
{\plain {with a real number tr called the
 }{\b threshold.}{ The }{\b decision rule}{
 we choose is this:\par \par       "D}{ecide
 a signal is present}{ if and only if the}{\b 
 test statistic}{ (31.7) exceeds tr ". }}
}
.TXT 9 0 360 0
Cg a60.500000,61.875000,373
{\rtf1\ansi \deff0
{\fonttbl
{\f0\fnil Arial;}
{\f1\fnil Symbol;}
}
{\plain {Now we re-examine our processor
 choice when (b,}{\f1 t}{\fs16 \dn6 o}{)
 is not (1,0); in particular, we examine
 it when the radar data is purely signal-
 that is, z(t) is replaced in (31.7) by 
bf(t-}{\f1 t}{\fs16 \dn6 o}{). Note that
 taking the magnitude in (31.7) means that
 b enters only as |b|: its unknown phase,
 of no interest to us anyway, does not enter
 the form.}}
}
.TXT 12 0 361 0
Cg a62.250000,62.000000,210
{\rtf1\ansi \deff0
{\fonttbl
{\f0\fnil Arial;}
{\f1\fnil Symbol;}
}
{\plain {\b Exercise 31.3}{  Referring to
 (31.7), show that if either the real or
 imaginary part is        used- instead 
of the magnitude- then the unknown phase
 would render the                procedure
 worthless.}}
}
.TXT 6 -11 545 0
C x1,1,0,0
.TXT 2 11 362 0
Cg a60.500000,61.000000,279
{\rtf1\ansi \deff0
{\fonttbl
{\f0\fnil Arial;}
{\f1\fnil Symbol;}
}
{\plain {If the delay is unknown, then our
 above reasoning leads us to compute a set
 of test statistics with h(t;}{\f1 t}{\fs16 
\dn6 o}{) = conj[f[-(t-}{\f1 t}{\fs16 \dn6 
o}{)]] for a sufficiently dense set of possible
 }{\f1 t}{\fs16 \dn6 o}{. That is, we must
 compute the set of forms }}
}
.EQN 11 10 364 0
((|((-{0:\¥}NAME&{0:\¥}NAME`{0:z}NAME({0:s}NAME)*({0:f}NAME(-({0:\t.o}NAME-{0:s}NAME)))]&{0:s}NAME))))^(2)
.TXT 0 47 366 0
Cg a4.125000,5.000000,9
{\rtf1\ansi \deff0
{\fonttbl
{\f0\fnil Arial;}
{\f1\fnil Symbol;}
}
{\plain {(31.8)}}
}
.TXT 8 -57 365 0
Cg a59.875000,61.000000,315
{\rtf1\ansi \deff0
{\fonttbl
{\f0\fnil Arial;}
{\f1\fnil Symbol;}
}
{\plain {But all these computations can 
be realized in a fairly simple way. We perform
 a matched filtering of the radar data and
 examine its output over a set of times 
equal to the set of selected delays. If 
the maximum value exceeds the threshold 
then we declare a signal present at the 
location of the maximum value.}}
}
.TXT 10 0 371 0
Cg a61.375000,61.125000,320
{\rtf1\ansi \deff0
{\fonttbl
{\f0\fnil Arial;}
{\f1\fnil Symbol;}
}
{\plain {Note that our processing scheme
 can, }{to a certain extent}{, handle the
 case of multiple objects with a modification
 of the decision rule. We decide an object
 present at every location whose output 
is above the threshold, assuming that signals
 are well separated, or resolved, with respect
 to the width of the IR.}}
}
.TXT 12 -11 369 0
Cg a33.375000,73.000000,46
{\rtf1\ansi \deff0
{\fonttbl
{\f0\fnil Arial;}
{\f1\fnil Symbol;}
}
{\plain {\b \fs24 Linear FM (Chirp) Pulse
 Modulation}}
}
.TXT 4 11 370 0
Cg a61.375000,61.000000,798
{\rtf1\ansi \deff0
{\fonttbl
{\f0\fnil Arial;}
{\f1\fnil Symbol;}
}
{\plain {A popular choice of radar pulse
 modulation has a phase modulation depending
 quadratically upon time. The derivative
 of the phase, the frequency modulation,
 is therefore linear in time. At audible
 frequencies such a modulation sounds like
 a chirp. This }{\b linear FM}{\b0 ,}{ or
 }{\b Chirp}{\b0 ,}{ modulation is useful
 because its bandwidth can be independent
 of its time extent, which is determined
 by the pulse amplitude modulation. Thus,
 under a peak power restraint to prevent
 physical damage, a pulse amplitude modulation
 can be chosen }{of sufficient time extent}{
 so the pulse modulation has the energy 
for good detection performance}{- recall
 Exercise 31.1-}{ and has large bandwidth
 for location accuracy and resolution. We
 wish to illustrate these attributes of 
this signal. }}
}
.TXT 24 0 624 0
Cg a61.375000,62.000000,353
{\rtf1\ansi \deff0
{\fonttbl
{\f0\fnil Arial;}
{\f1\fnil Symbol;}
}
{\plain {We choose a magnitude for the linear
 FM pulse so that we can easily perform 
all the calculations with pencil and paper.
 Better, w}{e employ the symbolic mathematics
 capability of Mathcad to good advantage-
 and leave the former efforts to problems
 as a check! For more practical magnitude
 models, the computations would likely be
 done numerically.}}
}
.TXT 12 0 372 0
Cg a42.625000,61.000000,58
{\rtf1\ansi \deff0
{\fonttbl
{\f0\fnil Arial;}
{\f1\fnil Symbol;}
}
{\plain {The linear FM pulse has a complex
 modulation modeled by}}
