{\plain {Examining the output, w}{\fs20 
\f0 e note again that, relative to the input,
 the output is of a larger peak value and
 a very narrow time extent- in other words,
 of  fine resolution. This is the great 
virtue of the linear FM signal. We see that
 the sampling procedure has introduced false
 images, whose location and peak value depend
 on}{\f0 \fs24  }{\f1 \fs24 f}{\f0 \fs24 
. }{\fs20 \f0 By trial and error we find
 that, with}{\f0 \fs24  }{\f1 \fs24 f}{=6.1,
 these false images have a relative size
 of -40 db: sufficiently low to be ignored.
 (We remark, however, that radar signals
 can show a dynamic range of more than 70
 db: that is, some weak signals of interest
 can be 70 db below the strongest signals)!}}
}
